huttar@hp750.itg.ti.com (Lars Huttar) writes:

>Hey, this would be cool...
>Could the game be changed to add a random "contrib" level branching
>off from some point in the dungeon?
>
>For example, level 10 might have two "up" staircases; one leads back
>to level 9, one to a randomly-selected "contrib" level that could be
>designed locally.  The contrib levels would be in their own
>subdirectory of nethackdir; perhaps a contrib.dir file in this
>subdirectory would be generated to keep track of the contrib levels
>and eliminate the need for nethack to scan for files.  If no contrib
>files were present, the top of the staircase would be "sealed".  But
>some contrib files would probably be included with the official
>distribution before long.

Spoilers:


There are two different utilities relevant here.  One is the level
compiler, lev_comp, which has a detailed grammar and lets you
build a single level in most any way you want (someone on the
D Team told me that there are some things you cannot build
yet which normally appear in NetHack, but it seems pretty
comprehensive -- mazes, corridor turnings, room types, altar
types, yes, engraved strings on the floor, etc.).  I've made
some levels successfully with this program and played them.

The second utility is dgn_comp, the dungeon compiler, which
controls the master flow and theme of the game.  This is basically
a list of "dungeons" (The Dungeons of Doom is the first one,
then the Gnomish Mines, etc.), and a description of ways
they are linked together and percentage chances of coming
across them.  Special levels, like the bigroom level and other
levels created with lev_comp, are given their relative places
in the dungeon by lines in this script, also.  For example,
from the script generated by dgn_comp (called "dungeon" in
the playground), the game learns that the bigroom special
level can be generated anywhere from levels 10 to 13 in the
Dungeons of Doom, and it has a 15% chance of being generated.
If the game does include the bigroom level, it reads in
the compiled bigroom level file created with lev_comp, also
located in the playground.

From personal experimentation and communication with the D Team,
the level compiler is much more usable than the dungeon compiler,
because at present the game still has too many compiled-in
notions about where it should find things.  I've been able
to link in a special level at level 2 of the Dungeons of Doom
with no problem.  But I couldn't create my own endgame, up
from level 1 of DoD (maybe I wasn't clever enough, though).

So, the way to add user-contributed levels will be to change
the master dungeon script and recompile it with dgn_comp.
One could add a line, say,

LEVEL:	"contrib" "C" @ (2, 0)

which would tell the game to search for a contributor level
in the file called "contrib", and this file could be changed
from time to time with different levels.

